  "query_preview": "..(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 306 PM EST SAT NOV 21 2026\n\nThe long-term period opens Monday morning with a noticeably cooler\nweather pattern underway as we head into the last full week of\nNovember. A 500 hPa trough will be pivoting across eastern Canada\nand the Great Lakes, with a dampening shortwave trough transiting\nthe Ohio Valley. At the surface, a corresponding ~995 mb surface \nlow will be shifting across Quebec, dragging a trailing cold front\ndraped from the Montreal area southwestward along the western \nslopes of the Appalachians, including across far southeastern \nKentucky. Upstream, an ~1034 mb surface high, centered near the \nNorth Dakota/Manitoba/Saskatchewan triple point, extends south \nacross the High Plains under a 500 hPa ridge that extends from the\nCentral Plains north and northwestward into the Yukon. Further \nwest, two distinct but potent shortwaves/vort maxes are noted: one\nover the Great Basin and a second over southern Alberta/southern \nBritish Columbia.\n\nThe models are in good agreement, showing the cold front \ndeparting to our south and east on Monday morning with any \nleftover light rain showers coming to an end. Total rainfall \namounts with the front appear quite light, as LREF probabilities \nfor at least 0.20 inches are under 15 percent area-wide. \nTemperatures will struggle to rise much on Monday afternoon as 925\nhPa temperatures fall from 4 to 10C (NW to SE) at 12Z Monday to -1\nto 1C by 12Z Tuesday on brisk northerly flow. A moderate surface  \npressure gradient will remain in place across the area on Tuesday \nbetween the departed cold front well to our south and the surface \nhigh passing over the Great Lakes. This will keep a chilly \nnortherly breeze going throughout the day, and MOS suggests \ntemperatures will struggle to reach 50F across most of the area.\n\nBy 00Z Wednesday, the two upstream shortwaves will have ejected\neast of the Rockies: the southern one traversing Texas as it skims\nclose to the Gulf of America, while the northern stream begins to\ndig southeast from Saskatchewan as a closed low. Model agreement\nseems to be consolidating on a solution that does bring us a cold,\nwetting rainfall late Wednesday into Thanksgiving Day, but many of\nthe details still remain uncertain as the degree and speed of\nnorthern- and southern-system phasing remains highly variable\nacross the 21/12Z model suite. Guidance suggests that as that first\nstorm system pulls away, troughing over the Ohio Valley will be\nreinforced by another northern-stream shortwave next weekend, \nthough significant model spread remains.\n\nIn sensible weather terms, look for any leftover rain showers to\ndepart on Monday morning, leaving a light breeze and a cooler\nMonday afternoon with highs mainly in the mid 50s. Chillier\ntemperatures follow on Monday night with most places seeing lows\nnear or below 32F. It is worth noting that the latest first 32F\nreading at JKL is November 24th. If freezing temperatures are not\nobserved Monday night into Tuesday morning, we will be in\nunrecorded territory as far as the latest first 32F reading at JKL\n(which is on a thermal belt ridge; most valley locations have\ndipped into the 20s and seen a killing freeze at this point).\nTuesday remains breezy and mostly sunny but cool, with highs only\nin the mid 40s to around 50F. Wetter weather then returns heading\ninto Wednesday and Thursday, and again potentially next weekend,\nwith temperatures expected to remain near- to slightly below-normal\nfor this time of year.",
